ches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h100","cnm_h040"],"id":"three-step-complex-power-cycle","label":"Find the repeating power cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"three-step-complex-power-cycle":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102","cnm_h097","cnm_h100","cnm_h040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 6","reason":"Set w = z+2i. The polynomial becomes w^3+w+10 = (w+2)(w^2-2w+5), with roots -2 and 1+/-2i. Thus the original roots are -2-2i, 1, and 1-4i. The last two form a vertical base of length 4, and the first has horizontal distance 3 from that base. The area is 4*3/2 = 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h100"],"id":"center-shift-and-factor","label":"Translate the variable and factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-shift-and-factor":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h100","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_h098","cnm_p148"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"center-shift-and-vieta","label":"Translate and use root identities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-shift-and-vieta":["cnm_h100","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h100","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 1 real pair and 2 nonreal complex pairs","reason":"Set t = x + 1. Then y = t² and ty = 1, so t³ = 1. Its roots are 1 and (-1 ± i√3)/2. The first gives (x,y) = (0,1), while the other two give nonreal pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Imaginary pairs in the historical choices means nonreal complex pairs, not pairs of purely imaginary coordinates."],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q43","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_h100"],"id":"substitute-the-repeated-expression","label":"Substitute the repeated expression","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-the-repeated-expression":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h097"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h097","cnm_148","cnm_h100"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: -a","reason":"For every root r, |r| = 1 implies 1/r = conjugate(r). Therefore the reciprocal sum is the conjugate of the root sum, which is conjugate(-a) = -a because a is real.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1987_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_032"],"id":"unit-circle-reciprocals","label":"Turn reciprocals into conjugates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unit-circle-reciprocals":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h103"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h103","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_032"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, the sum is -i, not 2","reason":"The root sum is -(-1)/i=-i. Also, the discriminant is 1-4i(2i)=9, and i(x-i)(x+2i)=0 gives roots i and -2i, confirming the other statements.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h100"],"id":"coefficient-sum-and-option-checks","label":"Compare coefficients and check the statements","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coefficient-sum-and-option-checks":["cnm_226","cnm_h097","cnm_224","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_226","cnm_h097","cnm_224","cnm_222","cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h100"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, at least one of a and b is nonreal","reason":"The denominators require a≠0 and a+b≠0. Cross-multiplication gives a²+ab+b²=0. For real a,b, this is (a+b/2)²+3b²/4=0, forcing the excluded pair (0,0). Complex solutions exist with b/a=(-1±i√3)/2, allowing either one or both variables to be nonreal.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q39","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_020","cnm_h100"],"id":"solution:exclude-real-pairs-by-squares:96c397f36e76","label":"Rule out real pairs using squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exclude-real-pairs-by-squares":["cnm_h097","cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_h102","cnm_156","cnm_020","cnm_h100"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: sqrt(2)","reason":"Let alpha=x+iy and gamma=u+iv. The imaginary parts give y+v=-1 and x+v=1, so x-y=2. Consequently |alpha|^2=x^2+y^2>=(x-y)^2/2=2, and |gamma|>=0. Taking alpha=1-i and gamma=0 attains sqrt(2), with f(1)=5 and f(i)=-3 both real.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_148"],"id":"solution:complete-square-modulus:90a7c5a2bd4f","label":"Bound the modulus by completing a square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-square-modulus":["cnm_h098","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235","cnm_h097","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_148"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_174"],"id":"nearest-point-on-line","label":"Find the closest possible point for alpha","methodDirectNodeIds":{"nearest-point-on-line":["cnm_h098","cnm_084","cnm_137","cnm_065"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_084","cnm_137","cnm_065","cnm_h097","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (92)","reason":"Set (u1,u2,u3,u4,u5)=(4-a,a-b,b-c,c-d,d), with sum 4. Since P(1)>0, a unit zero z is not 1. From (z-1)P(z)=0, obtain 4=sum uj·z^(-j). Equality in the unit-circle bound forces z^j=1 whenever uj>0. Thus positive weights are supported within {2,4}, {3}, or {5}. These yield P(1) values 8,10,12,14,16, plus 12 and 20. Their sum is 92.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_029"],"id":"coefficient-differences-and-real-parts","label":"Turn ordered coefficients into nonnegative differences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coefficient-differences-and-real-parts":["math:roots-of-unity","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_146","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, sqrt(2)","reason":"The terms begin 0, i, -1+i, -i, -1+i, -i. The recurrence therefore alternates between -1+i and -i from the third term onward. Since 111 is odd, z111=-1+i and its modulus is sqrt(2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h197","cnm_h097","cnm_174"],"id":"verify-the-two-term-cycle","label":"Verify the repeating cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"verify-the-two-term-cycle":["cnm_h200","math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_h100"]},"nodeIds":["